const usageBody=`<div class="grid cols-2"><div><h3>Build with the system.</h3><ul class="usage-list" style="margin-top:24px"><li><strong>01</strong><span>Import tokens and the local font files first. Keep one semantic color role per meaning.</span></li><li><strong>02</strong><span>Pick a page recipe, then choose each section’s own visual from the evidence library.</span></li><li><strong>03</strong><span>Use motion for emphasis or optional exploration. Keep payment, approval and evidence actions explicit.</span></li><li><strong>04</strong><span>Preserve the synthetic data model and labels. Readiness packets never imply posting or payment.</span></li><li><strong>05</strong><span>Review desktop, 390 px mobile, keyboard, zoom and reduced motion before shipping.</span></li></ul></div><div class="stack"><h4>A small technical footprint.</h4><p class="muted">Plain HTML and CSS own the interface. D3 owns twenty figure families across roughly 44 small SVG specimens in this reference. Labels are direct, data tables are available, and all assets/fonts are local. Filter state is temporary; nothing is sent, paid or written to an external system.</p><div class="code">&lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="tokens.css"&gt;<br>&lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="components.css"&gt;<br><br>&lt;button class="btn btn-flip"&gt;…&lt;/button&gt;<br>&lt;article class="card card-border"&gt;…&lt;/article&gt;<br><br>SVG figures: accessible title + text equivalent<br>Reduced motion: static, complete states</div><a href="brand-guide.html" class="btn btn-secondary">Read the complete guide ${I('arrow-up-right')}</a></div></div>`;
